#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481716} :activity/id #uuid "3c12d8df-59d7-51f9-9588-92c3d42e2238" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049696 :topic/id #uuid "0bfad6b0-12ee-58d9-8324-1f301647b82f" :topic/math-academy-id 1957} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479833 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002afc6b-3ae6-5e4d-9e87-6404be2be05d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479355} {:answer/id #uuid "a95258ee-6056-5703-bec4-79d09d5e79de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479610} {:answer/id #uuid "d3510268-8c4c-529e-8b03-2fd8c635e27b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479679} {:answer/id #uuid "dd42a3d6-7453-56cc-b6fd-7e6f716d0a3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479686} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc862f5-359e-590e-930c-858b88ff0363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479689}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479686} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff4a0306-bebb-5a0b-88e8-580bcd8a3741" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0c2a62d8-6d93-5394-bb23-b474c6edf899" :question/math-academy-id "q-65128" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x{e}^{xy}$\n\nIII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2y+1$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2y+1$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy} \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}(1+xy) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy} \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}(1+xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y+1)=2 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y+1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'."} :step/id #uuid "0330742a-33ff-5afc-89cc-0f92fa6fe78f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481724}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481716 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124999 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3e26fd3-6f10-57ac-9734-b2dbccddcc7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385358} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca4c3e9-3be8-5181-ba90-8364eb252764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385359} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d7bc4e-c57e-5018-af71-503efedc8f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385360} {:answer/id #uuid "be8924f2-9c7d-5be9-8109-8089f340a29f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385361} {:answer/id #uuid "2094e71e-1eb0-5d91-a373-c7101206e62e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385362}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385361} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6caccd5-2239-5eb0-a506-3902ad0cde8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8d93fe54-16d4-572d-b03f-303a7e6e19a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-36521" :question/problem "Suppose that a function $f(x,y,z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}=(x{y}^{2}+2y){e}^{xy}+2{z}^{3},\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂z}=6x{z}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluate the following expression at the point $(1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂y∂x}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nHence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂y∂x} & =\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x{y}^{2}+2y){e}^{xy}+2{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =(x{y}^{2}+2y)\\cdot x{e}^{xy}+(2xy+2)\\cdot {e}^{xy}+0 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4xy+2){e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂z}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(6x{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =12xz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate both partial derivatives at the point $(1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂y∂x}{|}_{(1,0,1)} & =(0+0+2){e}^{0}=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,0,1)} & =12(1)(1)=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂y∂x}{|}_{(1,0,1)}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,0,1)} & =2+12=14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7f5bbb0a-88b6-5f7d-9f06-8a26f6ee53a3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481675}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481724 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479870 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23f5ce90-f71e-52dc-8beb-408f7faeaa27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479415} {:answer/id #uuid "48a543dd-5073-5eff-a396-7a9535994086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479465} {:answer/id #uuid "4a8aa56b-9b6e-5e43-81d5-2db6976aeebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2+\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479471} {:answer/id #uuid "684701da-26b3-560c-8537-f88180932fce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479514} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb1a895-8ba1-56dc-9e30-7108e7d80a9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479711}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479415} :answer-field/id #uuid "50f2ed47-78e7-59d4-a40f-57fb0a2f9402" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "87b5b3e0-dcda-563d-b53d-0df7f66aab12" :question/math-academy-id "q-65129" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ evaluate ${f}_{yxy}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ if ${f}_{yy}(x,y)=-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$" :question/worked-solution "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yxy}(x,y) & ={f}_{yyx}(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{yy}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =-2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+(-{x}^{2})\\cdot (-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{yxy}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-2(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})+(1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\,\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a01dfe7c-004d-5b4b-a3ba-f0b7986950c5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481744}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481739 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409651 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4421a8c6-aea6-5d62-9cd0-8a06f8915dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409638} {:answer/id #uuid "45730328-f0dc-5c33-a6c2-53d71d04445d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409639} {:answer/id #uuid "4d58c242-f6fd-5d67-8e96-0273fcae35a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409641} {:answer/id #uuid "59fd5070-c975-5965-b9d4-d62ba5834853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409642} {:answer/id #uuid "d26ca855-4a28-50fd-bba1-040fec87c89e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409647}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409642} :answer-field/id #uuid "504a276b-9fed-5959-a86c-c2b987992901" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "923e8280-3481-5e90-8081-225a78ef2cea" :question/math-academy-id "q-133393" :question/problem "Evaluate ${f}_{zxz}(2,\\frac{1}{2},1)$ given that a function $f(x,y,z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n{f}_{zz}(x,y,z)=2z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)+x{e}^{z-2y}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{zzx}(x,y,z) & ={f}_{zxz}(x,y,z) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{zz}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)+x{e}^{z-2y}) \\\\  & =2z\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2y}+{e}^{z-2y} \\\\  & =\\frac{2z}{x-2y}+{e}^{z-2y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point  $(2,\\frac{1}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{zxz}(2,\\frac{1}{2},1) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{2-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}}+{e}^{1-2\\cdot 1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2-1}+{e}^{1-1} \\\\  & =2+1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dce668a8-ef22-5a60-bb9c-32f6ba050503"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481744 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad486eb1-3af5-599d-a9fa-a159211f22e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469206} {:answer/id #uuid "34be447a-67c4-5a73-8480-4c9e222a6cf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469207}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469206} :answer-field/id #uuid "25cbfe12-d294-5245-8577-5393288ad9f4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470215} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b396de7c-837a-5a29-89f1-49cbd1530aca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469208} {:answer/id #uuid "8cec73c4-a0c4-5d0f-b9fe-a203948ac871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469209}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469209} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8af40fc-fffa-522e-a59f-f12c36507ded" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470216} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcdf7232-c17f-5a4f-aed3-4cd3d5ebaa84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469210} {:answer/id #uuid "45e16277-28a2-510b-88f0-c7b6217947e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469211}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469211} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6521383-885b-5c99-af14-d69ae9a433ee" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0fedf8ed-0c48-5105-b489-f0efda76d273" :question/math-academy-id "q-80759" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n- **I.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{-2x}-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{-y}-2x$\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=5$\n- **III.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2xy$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{-2x}-y)=-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{-y}-2x)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+{y}^{2})=4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2xy) \\\\  & =2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y \\\\  & =4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'."} :step/id #uuid "efa110b9-bada-584b-9f76-02913ac29d55" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481739}}] :activity/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481568}]}
